        'en' => [
            'title'   => 'Laser cutting fabric and felt: cotton, denim, polyester, fleece',
            'slug'    => 'laser-cutting-fabric-and-felt',
            'excerpt' => 'Laser cutting fabric and felt for clean, fray-free edges: which textiles work, which never to cut, and which xTool laser suits patches, patterns and decor.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'Laser cutting fabric gives you precise shapes without a cutting die and without pressing on the material, and synthetics such as polyester, fleece and nylon come out with a sealed edge that doesn’t fray. Cotton, linen, denim and wool felt don’t melt; their edges just brown slightly. A CO₂ laser is best for large pattern pieces and light fabrics, while a diode laser also cuts felt, patches and dark fabrics well.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Below we explain which fabrics to cut, how to prepare the material and which xTool laser to choose: the CO₂ P3 or the S1, M2 or F2 diode laser. We finish with small business ideas, from laser cutting felt to patches and pattern pieces.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Why cut fabric with a laser?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Scissors and rotary cutters drag the fabric, and a cutting die only pays off for large runs. A laser cuts without contact, and you change the shape in a file. The main advantages:' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Synthetic edges don’t fray because they seal as they are cut, so there is no need to overlock or glue them.',
                    'Fine detail: letters, cut-outs and narrow bridges you can’t cut cleanly with scissors.',
                    'No die: a new shape is a new file, and the tenth patch is identical to the first.',
                    'Cutting and engraving in one job, for example a denim patch with an engraved logo.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'The downsides are the brownish edge on natural fabrics and the smoke and smell, which call for fume extraction. For thousands of identical pieces, a die is faster.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Which fabrics can you laser cut – and which never?' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Fabrics that handle the laser well' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Wool and synthetic felt both cut quickly and cleanly, which makes laser cutting felt the easiest place to start. Wool smells like burnt hair when cut.',
                    'Cotton and linen cut well, but the edge browns slightly.',
                    'Denim cuts like heavy cotton, and when engraved the laser lightens the indigo dye. Any elastane in it melts.',
                    'Polyester, fleece and nylon melt and seal at the edge.',
                    'Canvas, a dense cotton for bags, is cut fastest by a CO₂ laser; a diode needs more passes.',
                ] ],
                [ 'h3', 'What must never go under the laser' ],
                [ 'p', 'Materials containing PVC (polyvinyl chloride) release hydrogen chloride when lasered, which irritates the airways and corrodes the machine. So keep these away from the laser:' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'PVC-coated fabrics: tarpaulins, oilcloth, banner material and some rainwear fabrics.',
                    'PVC leatherette and vegan leather of unknown composition.',
                    'PVC heat transfer vinyl and flex films. Use only films the manufacturer describes as laser-safe.',
                    'Plastisol prints, because plastisol is PVC in a plasticiser.',
                    'PTFE-coated (Teflon) fabrics, whose fumes contain toxic fluorine compounds.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'Nylon and acrylic fibres can release hazardous gases during processing, including hydrogen cyanide. Do not assume a fan or purifier alone removes them: check material composition, instructions and extraction suited to the hazards before processing; if adequate protection cannot be provided, do not cut. Never use materials of unknown composition.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Flammability: loose fibres catch fire easily' ],
                [ 'p', 'Cotton and linen ignite easily, especially loose threads, fluff, flannel and terry cloth, and scraps under the fabric can keep smouldering after the cut. Clear offcuts from the bed before every job and never leave a running machine unattended. There is more in our article on working safely with a laser.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Natural vs synthetic edges: scorched or melted?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Cotton, linen, wool, silk and viscose don’t melt. The laser vaporises the fibres and chars their ends, so the edge is dry and slightly brown, clearly visible on light material. Higher speed, lower power and air assist reduce scorching, but you usually won’t get a perfectly clean edge on white cotton, and loosely woven fabrics can fray over time.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Polyester, fleece, nylon and polypropylene melt at the edge and the fibres fuse together. That is the laser’s biggest advantage for patches, ribbons and appliqués. With too much power, though, the seal turns hard and scratchy, which is uncomfortable in clothing, so leave a seam allowance on pattern pieces. Poly-cotton blends seal only partly.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Engraving denim, fleece and felt' ],
                [ 'p', 'On denim, low power lightens the indigo dye and creates a worn look similar to the laser finishing used in the jeans industry. That way you can engrave a name or logo on a jacket or bag, but too much energy burns straight through. On fleece the laser melts the pile and leaves an embossed pattern; on felt it leaves a shallow, darker recess.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Start with a test grid on an offcut of the same material. Some xTool Selected materials, such as felt and a denim bag, come with a QR code holding recommended settings, which camera-equipped P and F series machines read automatically.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'How do you hold fabric flat and avoid scorching?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Fabric ripples and lifts in the air stream, and then the beam loses focus and scorches more. So:' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Iron the fabric, because creases change the focus.',
                    'Secure the fabric with suitable clamps or weights outside the cut path. Do not stretch knits, or the cut shape may shrink afterwards.',
                    'Magnets hold only on a magnetic base such as steel. On the P3 aluminium panel, use suitable clamps or weights that stay clear of the beam path.',
                    'On the P3, lay all the bed slats. For material 1 mm thick or thinner xTool recommends the full set (the P3 comes with 17) so the material doesn’t vibrate or ripple.',
                    'Stiffen thin fabric with interfacing, and cut appliqués with double-sided fusible web already ironed on.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'For the M2, xTool offers the CleanCut panel, designed for materials such as felt. According to xTool, it protects the base plate from burn marks and scratches when cutting through and improves smoke extraction for a smooth edge.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Air assist blows smoke and flame away, so the edge browns less, but too strong a stream lifts thin fabric. The P3 has its own air pump, and for the S1 xTool sells the Smart Air Assist. Lower power at higher speed, two fast passes instead of one slow one and focus set exactly on the fabric surface also help.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Which xTool laser for fabric and felt?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Wavelength affects processing: a CO₂ laser uses infrared light (10.6 µm), while a diode laser typically uses blue light (about 455 nm). Results also depend on composition, pigment, thickness and fabric structure. Light materials can absorb blue light poorly; choose settings for the specific material and test first.' ],
